#. <terms> links to the Terms, <privacy> to the privacy note.
msgctxt "pages.rules.author.also"
msgid "These rules are part of the <terms>Terms</terms>, which say what Gray Horizon is and what happens to your account. The <privacy>privacy note</privacy> says what is kept about you."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.author.title"
msgid "Who wrote this"
msgstr ""

#. {author} is a person's name and stays as it is; {email} is an email address drawn as a link.
msgctxt "pages.rules.author.who"
msgid "<b>{author}</b>, reachable at {email}. This is a hobby project run by one person, not a company."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.broken.p1"
msgid "Sanctions run from a warning, through having something taken down, through being stopped from commenting, messaging, publishing, trading or changing your name and banner, up to being suspended for a while and having the account closed for good."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.broken.p2"
msgid "<b>You are told.</b> Nobody is punished quietly here. When something is refused, the refusal says what you cannot do, why, and until when, and everything standing against you is listed on your own profile. A reason can be held back in the narrow case where giving it would help somebody get around a check, and when that happens you are told it is being held back rather than left to think there was none."
msgstr ""

#. An appeal is the player's one request that a different moderator look again at a decision.
msgctxt "pages.rules.broken.p3"
msgid "<b>You can ask for a second look.</b> A suspension, a closure and any restriction that stops you doing something can be appealed once, within fourteen days of the notice, and never by the person who decided it. A warning stops nothing, so there is nothing there to appeal. The sanction stays in force while the appeal is read, because an appeal that paused it would be the quickest way to carry on doing the thing you were stopped for."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.broken.title"
msgid "If a rule is broken"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.buy.p1"
msgid "This one is a promise to you rather than a restriction on you. <b>Real money can never buy power in Gray Horizon.</b> Not currency, not energy, not experience, not production, not equipment that hits harder, not better odds from a case, not a vote and not a strategic weapon."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.buy.p2"
msgid "What money will ever be able to buy is how you look, and conveniences that do not change what you can do: a frame, a banner, a title that is clearly not an earned one, a skin on equipment that behaves exactly the same. Anything bought stays with the account that bought it, so there is no route back from money into the game’s economy."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.buy.p3"
msgid "During this alpha nothing is for sale at all. There is a tip jar, and it buys nothing, unlocks nothing and changes nothing about how the game treats you, which is the point."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.buy.title"
msgid "Nothing you can buy makes you stronger"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.automation"
msgid "<b>Automation.</b> Scripts, bots, macros and anything else that plays for you. Working, training, fighting, voting and opening cases automatically are not allowed at all. The public interface developers can build on reads the world and never changes it: there is no permission to be granted that lets software act for you, and every attempt is written down."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.bug"
msgid "<b>Using a bug instead of telling us.</b> If the game gives you something it clearly did not mean to, using it is cheating. Telling us is how it gets fixed, and the link for that is in the alpha notice at the top of the game."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.intro"
msgid "Cheating is getting something the game did not mean to give you, or putting more than one person’s effort behind one citizen. These are the ones worth naming."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.outro"
msgid "Taking somebody’s goods and not paying is not cheating the game, but it is reportable and the same people look at it. Anything a player does can be reported, not only the things a player writes."
msgstr ""

#. The four items below are bullets; each starts with a bold name.
msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.sharing"
msgid "<b>Sharing an account, or playing somebody else’s.</b> An account has to be controlled by one person and nobody else."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.title"
msgid "What counts as cheating"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.cheating.vote"
msgid "<b>Manipulating a vote.</b> Accounts made in order to vote, and any other arrangement where one person casts more than one ballot. Ballots stay secret from governments; staff can still investigate fraud, and a result found to be fraudulent can have those ballots struck and be certified again."
msgstr ""

#. {version}: the version number, such as 1.1. The date is the day this version was written.
msgctxt "pages.rules.deck"
msgid "These are the rules everybody in Gray Horizon is held to, and the ones moderators act on. This is version {version}, last updated on 4 October 2026; your account records the version you accepted when you registered. It was written in plain language by the person who runs the game, and it is not a lawyer’s document."
msgstr ""

#. {version} is the version of the rules a player accepts, such as 1.1.
msgctxt "pages.rules.eyebrow"
msgid "Version {version}"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.human.p1"
msgid "One person plays one account, and that account is one citizen. There are no second characters, no slots and no starting again. The citizen you made is the one you keep, whatever country it ends up in and whatever it ends up doing."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.human.p2"
msgid "A second account is not allowed, whatever it is for: not to vote with, not to open cases with, not to fight with, and not to hold things you would rather were not on your own citizen."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.human.p3"
msgid "Several people in one house, one university, one office or one network can all play. The rule is <b>one account each, controlled by that person alone.</b> Coming from the same address is never treated on its own as proof of anything, and nobody is asked for identity documents in order to play."
msgstr ""

#. Closing every account a person holds is permanent: it cannot be undone.
msgctxt "pages.rules.human.p4"
msgid "Where somebody is found running more than one account, <b>all of them are closed permanently</b>, not only the spare. This rule is about a person, not about a login, and closing only the second one would mean losing the thing you were never allowed to have while the account it was feeding kept everything it had been fed."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.human.p5"
msgid "Along with automating the game, this is the wrong that closing an account for good mainly exists for. It takes real evidence, and you are told and can ask for a second look, the same as with anything else below."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.human.title"
msgid "One human, one citizen"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.metaDescription"
msgid "The rules every Gray Horizon player is held to: one human, one citizen, what counts as cheating, how to behave where players write to each other, and what happens when a rule is broken."
msgstr ""

#. World Rules The rules every player is held to, and the ones moderators act on. They are game rules, not a legal contract, so they are translated. Keep each tag where the words it wraps are: <b>…</b> is bold, <link>…</link> is a link.
msgctxt "pages.rules.metaTitle"
msgid "World Rules | Gray Horizon"
msgstr ""

#. Times are Central European time; keep "18:00 and 22:00" and "24 hours" as numbers.
msgctxt "pages.rules.reporting.p1"
msgid "There is a Report control on a citizen’s profile, on an article, on a comment, on a private message and on an organisation’s page. Using it makes the game take its own copy of what you are reporting, so deleting it afterwards does not make it go away. During the alpha one person reads the reports, every evening between 18:00 and 22:00 Central European time, and deals with each one within 24 hours."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.reporting.p2"
msgid "A report is never shown to the person it is about, and a sanction names the rule and the conduct rather than naming you."
msgstr ""

#. The link goes to the public record of what moderators did.
msgctxt "pages.rules.reporting.record"
msgid "See <link>what moderation did, month by month</link>."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.reporting.title"
msgid "Reporting somebody"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.title"
msgid "World Rules"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.p1"
msgid "You can write in articles, in the comments under them, in messages, in chat, and in the names and descriptions you give a party, a company, a bank, a newspaper, a military unit or an organisation. All of it is somewhere another player reads, and all of it can be reported."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.p2"
msgid "Gray Horizon is a political game, and politics in it is allowed to be hostile. Arguing about ideology, including about fascism and extremism as subjects, is part of what the game is for. Using the game to glorify or recruit for an extremist movement is not, and neither is targeted hate, a credible threat, publishing somebody’s real details, sexual content involving minors, or scamming somebody."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.p3"
msgid "Your country cannot overrule that. A government can be as authoritarian as its players vote for, and can censor its own state spaces if that is the politics it wants, but no office and no roleplay reaches these rules. <b>A president cannot ban somebody from Gray Horizon.</b>"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.p4"
msgid "A newspaper cannot silence the argument under its own article either. The only person who can take a comment down is the person who wrote it."
msgstr ""

#. Mute hides a person's writing from you; block stops them writing to you.
msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.p5"
msgid "You can mute somebody, which hides what they write from you, and block them, which stops them writing to you. Neither is a punishment and neither needs anybody’s permission."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "pages.rules.writing.title"
msgid "Writing to each other"
msgstr ""

